---
import HardwareNote from "../../layouts/HardwareNote.astro";
import { url, evidenceUrl } from "../../lib/specs";
import HwSection from "../../components/HwSection.astro";
import HwNote from "../../components/HwNote.astro";

// From docs/evidence/touch-reports.md: evtest, a finger dragged on the glass.
// <b> marks the lines that distinguish "a device node exists" from "the
// controller is scanning".
const evtest = `Event: time ..., type 3 (EV_ABS), code 57 (ABS_MT_TRACKING_ID), value 27
<b>Event: time ..., type 3 (EV_ABS), code 53 (ABS_MT_POSITION_X), value 456</b>
<b>Event: time ..., type 3 (EV_ABS), code 54 (ABS_MT_POSITION_Y), value 888</b>
Event: time ..., type 3 (EV_ABS), code 48 (ABS_MT_TOUCH_MAJOR), value 3
<b>Event: time ..., type 1 (EV_KEY), code 330 (BTN_TOUCH), value 1</b>
Event: time ..., type 3 (EV_ABS), code 0 (ABS_X), value 456
Event: time ..., type 3 (EV_ABS), code 1 (ABS_Y), value 888
Event: time ..., -------------- SYN_REPORT ------------`;
---

<HardwareNote
  slug="touch-reports"
  title="The touch controller was awake the whole time"
  eyebrow="LILYGO T-Display-K230 · hardware note · observed"
  dek="The GT9895 bound, probed, and created <code>/dev/input/event0</code> while reporting nothing at all. Every status check passed. The fault was one word in the device tree: the interrupt was declared edge&#8209;triggered, and a Goodix Berlin part holds its interrupt line low until it is acknowledged."
  description="On 2026-09-21 the GT9895 touch controller on the LILYGO T-Display-K230 reported multitouch under Linux. The interrupt type was the whole problem: 0 interrupts edge-triggered, 2173 level-triggered."
  source="docs/evidence/touch-reports.md"
  meta={[
    { label: "Observed", value: "2026-09-21<br>evtest and /proc/interrupts, on the board" },
    { label: "Controller", value: "Goodix GT9895 on i2c&#8209;1, 0x5d<br>interrupt GPIO23, reset GPIO24" },
    { label: "The fix", value: "one device-tree word<br><code>IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW</code>, no kernel change" },
    { label: "Reported grid", value: "1024&times;2400, the digitizer's own<br>against a 568&times;1232 panel" },
    { label: "Not settled", value: "whether the axes are swapped<br>or mirrored against the screen" },
  ]}
>
  <HwSection kind="the measurement" title="Zero, against two thousand one hundred and seventy-three">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Everything that is easy to check was passing. The driver bound at
      <code>i2c 1-005d</code>, <code>goodix_berlin</code> probed,
      <code>/dev/input/event0</code> appeared, and the part answered
      32&#8209;bit&#8209;addressed i2c reads. None of that is the same as a
      controller that is scanning the glass, which is the whole lesson of this
      board and the reason this site exists.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th><code>interrupts = &lt;23 &hellip;&gt;</code></th>
            <th>what changed</th>
            <th><code>/proc/interrupts</code></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING</code> (0x02)</th>
            <td class="remark">nothing else &mdash; same kernel, same image</td>
            <td><b>0</b></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW</code> (0x08)</th>
            <td class="remark">the device tree word, and only that</td>
            <td class="ours"><b>2173</b> over one 45 s capture</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <pre class="hw-code">87:   <b>2173</b>   gpio-k230  23  <b>Level</b>   goodix-berlin</pre>

    <p class="hw-p">
      That line is read back off the running board, so the setting under test
      is the setting that was live &mdash; not the one the source says should
      have been pushed. The gap between the two is not hypothetical here: the
      clock patch described in the <a href={url("hardware/panel-lit/")}>panel
      note</a> was built, shipped and never looked at, and it had blanked the
      screen.
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the cause" title="A trigger type cloned from a different part">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Our touch node was cloned from the Canaan reference board's, which
      describes an Edtech FT5306 &mdash; a different controller that happens to
      sit on the same pins. The comment in our device tree said as much. The
      trigger type came along for the ride and was never derived from any
      Goodix source.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code">touchscreen@38 &#123;
    compatible = "edt,edt-ft5306";        <i>/* not what is on this board */</i>
    reg = &lt;0x38&gt;;
    reset-gpios = &lt;&amp;gpio0_ports 24 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW&gt;;
    interrupt-parent = &lt;&amp;gpio0_ports&gt;;
    interrupts = &lt;23 <b>IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING</b>&gt;;   <i>/* inherited */</i>
&#125;;</pre>

    <HwNote kind="soc" label="Why edge does not merely under-report — inferred, not measured">
      <p>
        A Goodix Berlin part asserts its interrupt low and <em>holds</em> it
        until the host clears the status byte. The handler clears that byte
        only on its <code>out_clear</code> path, and its very first check
        returns early when <code>status</code> reads zero &mdash; without
        clearing. One interrupt taken at the wrong moment therefore leaves the
        line stuck low forever, and an edge detector never sees another falling
        edge. A level detector re-fires immediately and the handler eventually
        clears it: self-healing, which is why level is correct for a
        hold&#8209;until&#8209;acknowledged interrupt.
      </p>
      <p>
        This mechanism is read out of the driver source, not observed. It
        explains the counter freezing, and nothing rests on it: what settles
        the question is the two rows in the table above, measured on the board
        with one word changed between them.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="what it reports" title="Multitouch, with the tracking IDs to prove it">
    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={evtest} />

    <p class="hw-p">
      Tracking IDs are allocated and retired, <code>BTN_TOUCH</code> goes 1 and
      0, and <code>ABS_MT_TOUCH_MAJOR</code> ramps as the contact patch grows.
      A slot&#8209;0 trajectory decoded from a 60 s capture, with
      <code>ABS_MT_SLOT</code> honoured, spans X 86..728 and Y 765..1765
      continuously: both axes move, and the motion is smooth rather than
      quantised to a handful of values. That is the difference between a device
      node and a digitizer.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>axis</th><th>min</th><th>max</th><th>panel</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>ABS_X</code>, <code>ABS_MT_POSITION_X</code></th>
            <td>0</td><td>1023</td><td class="remark">568 pixels wide</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><code>ABS_Y</code>, <code>ABS_MT_POSITION_Y</code></th>
            <td>0</td><td>2399</td><td class="remark">1232 pixels tall</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="hw-p">
      <strong>The two grids do not match, and the driver cannot make them
      match.</strong> <code>goodix_berlin</code> reports through
      <code>touchscreen_report_pos()</code>, which applies the swap and invert
      properties and does <em>not</em> scale;
      <code>touchscreen-size-x</code> / <code>touchscreen-size-y</code> only
      declare the advertised maximum, and the controller's raw values pass
      through unchanged. So the input device speaks the digitizer's native
      1024&times;2400 and anything consuming it scales by 568/1024 and
      1232/2400. That is the ordinary Linux arrangement, and it is worth
      knowing before something lands a touch a factor of two away from the
      finger.
    </p>

    <p class="hw-p">
      Declaring those two properties was itself a fix. Without them the driver
      advertises <code>0 .. SZ_64K - 1</code> on both axes for a sensor that
      reports 0..1023 and 0..2399 &mdash; a client scaling by the advertised
      maximum would have collapsed every touch into the top-left corner.
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="what is not known" title="A drag proves movement, not direction">
    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="Whether the axes are swapped or mirrored">
      <p>
        Every capture so far is of undirected movement. A finger dragged around
        the glass proves the coordinates move and move smoothly; it cannot
        prove they move the right way. Settling it needs a touch at a
        <em>known</em> screen position &mdash; <code>tools/touch-axis-test.py</code>
        draws four coloured corner targets and reports which corner each
        contact fell in by its reported coordinates.
      </p>
      <p>
        It was blocked on the panel rather than on the touch controller: the
        image was sheared by the pixel&#8209;clock workaround described in the
        <a href={url("hardware/panel-lit/")}>panel note</a>, so a target drawn
        on screen was not where it appeared to be. That image has since been
        corrected and the shear is gone; the transcript still does not exist.
        Until it does this stays unverified, and the in-flight requirement
        carries a marker saying so.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="ours" label="The scare in the middle, and why it was not trusted">
      <p>
        Between the fix and the result above there was a capture that showed
        <code>Level</code> in <code>/proc/interrupts</code> and a count still at
        zero, with the touch-data register byte&#8209;for&#8209;byte identical
        eight seconds apart. It looked like a second, deeper fault.
      </p>
      <p>
        It was recorded as untrustworthy at the time, for a specific reason: it
        assumed a finger was on the panel during the window, and that was never
        confirmed. Three earlier conclusions on this project had already failed
        in exactly that way &mdash; DSI writes taken as delivery, a faithful
        transcription of dead code, and a bound driver taken as a working one.
        Redone with the press confirmed, the counter read 2173.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="No touch requirement is in the ledger">
      <p>
        The count on the <a href={url("")}>front page</a> does not include
        touch. Requirements reach that count when a change is archived into
        <code>openspec/specs/</code>, and this work is still in flight. What is
        on this page is an observation with a file behind it, which is not the
        same thing as a requirement the project has committed to.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the cost of the pin" title="Mainline has never supported this part">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      The pinned Xuantie kernel is 6.6 and carries
      <code>drivers/input/touchscreen/goodix.c</code>, which serves the older
      GT9xx generation. <code>goodix_berlin</code> landed in v6.9, and even at
      v6.18 the i2c driver matches only <code>goodix,gt9916</code> while the
      binding enumerates <code>gt9897</code> and <code>gt9916</code>. Mainline
      has never supported the GT9895 at any version.
    </p>
    <p class="hw-p">
      So the driver is carried here as an explicit backport under
      <code>nix/patches/goodix-berlin/</code>, and the Berlin programming model
      turned out to cover this part unchanged &mdash; the register layout was
      checked byte&#8209;for&#8209;byte against LilyGO's own RT&#8209;Smart
      <code>gt9895.c</code>, which performs no initialisation the backport is
      missing. Nothing in the kernel needed fixing. The cost of the pin is one
      backport to carry forward, recorded so that it is visible the next time
      the kernel revision moves.
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <div class="hw-sources">
    <p>
      Evidence: <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/touch-reports.md")}><code>docs/evidence/touch-reports.md</code></a>,
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/gt9895-port.md")}><code>docs/evidence/gt9895-port.md</code></a>,
      <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/touch-probe.txt")}><code>docs/evidence/touch-probe.txt</code></a>,
      and <a href={evidenceUrl("docs/evidence/gt9895-touch.md")}><code>docs/evidence/gt9895-touch.md</code></a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Source read for this note: the backported
      <code>goodix_berlin_core.c</code> under <code>nix/patches/</code>,
      <code>drivers/gpio/gpio-k230.c</code> and
      <code>arch/riscv/boot/dts/canaan/k230-canmv-v3-lcd.dts</code> in the
      pinned tree, and LilyGO's RT&#8209;Smart <code>gt9895.c</code>. The
      vendor trees are not committed here, so those paths are named and not
      linked.
    </p>
  </div>
</HardwareNote>
